WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.875
- When I'm drawing,

00:00:00.875 --> 00:00:04.129
It's one of the rare times
where I'm kind of focused and

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:06.631
feel like the world
slows down.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.761
- I'm Joel Crawford, the director
of Puss in Boots: The Last Wish. 

00:00:11.761 --> 00:00:12.679
- Hi, I'm Domee Shi,

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:14.848
the director
and co-writer of Turning Red.

00:00:14.848 --> 00:00:16.057
- I'm Chris Williams.
I’m the writer

00:00:16.057 --> 00:00:17.434
and director of The Sea Beast.

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:18.518
- My name is João Gonzalez.

00:00:18.518 --> 00:00:20.395
I'm the director,
animator, writer

00:00:20.395 --> 00:00:21.896
and composer of
Ice Merchants,

00:00:21.896 --> 00:00:23.898
and today I'm going to be
drawing my characters,

00:00:23.898 --> 00:00:24.774
receiving an Oscar.

00:00:24.774 --> 00:00:25.692
- And today
I'm going to draw

00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:27.652
Jacob Holland
winning an Oscar.

00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:28.653
- And today
I'm going to be drawing

00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:30.405
Puss in Boots,
receiving an Oscar.

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:31.614
- I will be drawing

00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:35.076
my character, Meilin Lee
receiving an Oscar.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.246
My definitive drawing
point would be like the head

00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:41.458
the tilt, the angle
and kind of like the

00:00:41.458 --> 00:00:43.334
expression of the character.

00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:45.795
- I find it's usually good
to start with the cranium,

00:00:46.004 --> 00:00:47.047
figure out that line of action

00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:48.173
through the middle
of the character.

00:00:48.173 --> 00:00:49.466
- What I love
about Puss in Boots

00:00:49.466 --> 00:00:51.342
is that there's this charisma,

00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:55.013
but underneath it all, he's
a cat that's wearing boots.

00:00:55.388 --> 00:00:57.849
Maybe the light that's
reflecting off of the Oscar

00:00:57.849 --> 00:00:59.934
creating kind of like
a laser pointer effect,

00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:02.103
would totally distract
Puss in Boots,

00:01:02.103 --> 00:01:06.691
and he can't help but
c-c-c-cat like chase after it.

00:01:06.691 --> 00:01:08.234
- So with these characters,
for me,

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.820
the most important part
was really to emphasize

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:12.989
the father son relationship.

00:01:12.989 --> 00:01:13.531
That's why

00:01:13.531 --> 00:01:16.785
I believe I also chose to exaggerate their proportions.

00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:17.452
For example,

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:18.995
the father having very,
very long

00:01:18.995 --> 00:01:21.247
legs and the kid being
quite small.

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:23.917
It was very important
for me to establish that

00:01:23.917 --> 00:01:25.960
very nice relationship
they have going.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:27.128
- It was a challenge for us,

00:01:27.128 --> 00:01:30.090
especially in this movie,
because a lot of things

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.925
that are associated
with being a tween

00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.427
girl are very expensive
and complicated to do.

00:01:34.469 --> 00:01:37.514
Backpacks are incredibly
difficult and expensive

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:41.684
to simulate, her tamagotchi
on her backpack.

00:01:41.851 --> 00:01:44.145
I definitely fought
for that Tamagotchi.

00:01:44.145 --> 00:01:45.730
- One of the things
we really wanted to land

00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:48.817
with Jacob as a character
is that he's a really good

00:01:48.817 --> 00:01:49.692
hearted guy.

00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:52.487
All right, so there's my under
drawing, so now I'll go ahead,

00:01:53.738 --> 00:01:55.615
and do the ink drawing.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:58.993
- The eyes are so important.
They're so expressive,

00:01:58.993 --> 00:02:02.080
and cats have that kind of way
where when they're focused

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:05.959
on something, they kind of
get this, like, tunnel vision,

00:02:05.959 --> 00:02:07.252
and it's fun creating that,

00:02:07.252 --> 00:02:09.462
on like an animated character.

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:10.046
- This is probably

00:02:10.046 --> 00:02:11.840
my favorite
phase of the drawing,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.675
which is when I'm
adding shadows.

00:02:13.675 --> 00:02:15.009
Not only because I
like how they look,

00:02:15.009 --> 00:02:16.845
esthetically, but
they're also perfect

00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:19.180
to cover some mistakes
that I've done.

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.641
- Sometimes when I'm drawing
a character's expression,

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:24.686
I can't help but
like try to make it myself,

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:27.480
they're all giggling
at the Oscar’s butt.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.066
That's the idea
for this drawing.

00:02:30.066 --> 00:02:31.776
- And one of things that I love
about animation

00:02:31.776 --> 00:02:33.820
is how deeply collaborative
it is.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.781
Hundreds of people
working for years on this

00:02:36.781 --> 00:02:37.657
one thing.

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:41.578
- So excited about the Oscar
that he's leaping forward.

00:02:45.123 --> 00:02:46.457
- Now I'm just noodling.

00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:48.668
Okay, I'm done.

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:50.461
- I drew Jacob Holland,

00:02:50.461 --> 00:02:52.213
one of the main characters
from The Sea Beast,

00:02:52.213 --> 00:02:55.175
holding an Oscar.
He seems quite happy with it.

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:57.260
- This image was
what came to my mind.

00:02:57.260 --> 00:02:59.387
In my mind,
it was father holding his son

00:02:59.387 --> 00:03:01.139
and the son holding the Oscar.

00:03:01.139 --> 00:03:03.266
So it was, it's
a double victory for him.

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:06.102
- Meilin would be over
the moon.

00:03:06.102 --> 00:03:09.689
She would be so excited
to be holding that heavy,

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:11.024
naked gold man

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.735
she would have poofed into
a panda out of excitement.

00:03:13.735 --> 00:03:16.362
- Over 400 people
worked on this movie,

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:20.116
and that means so much
that it's being appreciated,

00:03:20.116 --> 00:03:22.327
not just out in the world,
in theaters,

00:03:22.785 --> 00:03:25.330
but also by The Academy.

00:03:26.122 --> 00:03:27.248
So thank you.

